WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.669
Speaker 1: Andrew Yang was interviewed on
CNN about his new third party, the forward

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:04.890
party.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:08.090
It really went very, very poorly for Andrew
Yang.

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:13.730
And I want to kind of make clear as we get
into these clips, I like Andrew Yang on a

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.160
personal level.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.790
He's been a guest on this program many times.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:21.020
I was interviewing Andrew Yang before almost
anybody else.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.140
It might have been Sam Harris who interviewed
Yang before I did as far as national shows,

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.770
but we were early to talk to Andrew Yang.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:28.840
Universal basic income, etc.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.800
I like the guy.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.410
This interview was a disaster.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:36.140
Now people who emailed me had two different
points of view.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.990
Some, like me, believe that this interview
was a complete and total disaster because

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:48.340
Andrew Yang continues to just not explain
what are the policies of the forward party.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.940
Others felt that Jim Acosta, the interviewer,
bullied Andrew Yang and did not treat him

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:53.940
well.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:58.950
I just don't see that Andrew Yang in the forward
party keeps saying we're not left or right

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:05.680
but forward, and we take the common sense
consensus view on all of these divisive issues.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.680
Right.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.060
But you got to explain what the position is.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:11.350
And you can't just be like, well, anything
goes here because then you're not really a

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.350
party.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.220
I believe Jim Acosta did a good job in this
interview.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.220
Let's take a look at some clips.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.560
I think this is very bad for Andrew Yang.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.140
And as I've said before, to me, the Democratic
Party is too centrist.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:26.410
So when I think of a third party, which I
love, I want third parties.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:27.490
We need more than two parties.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:32.110
And we need to figure out the right way to
properly bolster and inject third parties

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:33.640
into the national dialog.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.760
I want something to the left of the Democratic
Party.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.440
Andrew Yang started the forward party with
some Republicans and it looks like they're

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.560
basically trying to place it between the Democratic
and Republican parties where there is relatively

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.530
little space, by the way, and it just it's
not working for me.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.119
Let's take a listen to the first part of this.

00:01:52.119 --> 00:01:53.280
Speaker 2: Interview on both sides.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.650
You're going to have to come up with policy.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:55.650
Speaker 3: Results, right?

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:56.650
But is it a better system?

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:01.049
Speaker 2: Yeah, but Andrew, you're going
to have to have policy positions at some point.

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:05.119
How does the forward party feel about Roe
versus Wade should have been overturned?

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:11.999
Speaker 3: Well, I personally think that women's
reproductive rights are fundamental human

00:02:11.999 --> 00:02:12.999
rights.

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:17.889
But the forward party has not left or right,
but forward stands on even the most divisive

00:02:17.889 --> 00:02:18.889
and contentious issue.

00:02:18.889 --> 00:02:19.920
Speaker 2: What does that mean?

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:20.920
Yeah.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.639
Speaker 1: What what does that mean?

00:02:22.639 --> 00:02:26.959
I mean, that that really doesn't make any
sense.

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:29.900
His personal view is not really the issue.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:36.150
You have a party here, and if the idea is,
well, rabid, anti-choicers are welcome as

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.590
well as anybody else.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.629
But we're going to take a consensus view.

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:41.239
Does that make any sense?

00:02:41.239 --> 00:02:42.730
And what is the consensus view?

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:43.730
You've got to explain it.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.329
Speaker 2: To take a position on something.

00:02:45.329 --> 00:02:48.239
You don't take a position on something.

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:51.371
You can't just say, well, you know, this is
a hot button issue, so I'm not going to take

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:52.439
a position on you.

00:02:52.439 --> 00:02:55.340
You know, you want to run the country, you're
going to have to make some hard decisions.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:56.340
Andrew.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.779
Speaker 3: Again, the forward party is about
that common sense consensus majority view,

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:02.360
which is very clear on abortion.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.989
Speaker 2: It's not about guns.

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:06.980
Speaker 1: Right now, Andrew Yang is correct.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:13.230
There's a very clear consensus view on abortion
by a higher percentage than at any point during

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.389
the Roe v Wade era.

00:03:15.389 --> 00:03:17.919
Americans believe abortion should be legal
in most cases.

00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:20.309
That's the common sense consensus view.

00:03:20.309 --> 00:03:24.430
But can he not say that because the forward
party is also filled with as many or even

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.900
more Republicans than Democrats?

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:27.900
I don't know.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.219
But this isn't really doing it for me.

00:03:29.219 --> 00:03:30.629
Speaker 2: What about assault weapons?

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:33.409
Speaker 3: It's actually clear on just about
every issue under the sun.

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:35.510
Should 18 year olds we should go by?

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.819
The teens should because of the nature of
our system.

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:39.870
Speaker 2: Should 18 year olds be able to
buy 15?

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:46.559
Speaker 3: Again, the common sense consensus
majority is that there should be some rules

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:49.169
around background checks and access to firearms.

00:03:49.169 --> 00:03:52.780
But we're not getting any of these things,
Jim, because the two party system does not

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:53.780
need to deliver.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.109
Speaker 2: It is sound like you're taking
any of the hard positions.

00:03:56.109 --> 00:03:57.109
It sounds like you're.

00:03:57.109 --> 00:03:58.109
Speaker 3: In.

00:03:58.109 --> 00:03:59.109
Speaker 2: Power.

00:03:59.109 --> 00:04:02.620
It sounds like you're sort of a fill in the
blank part of, you know, if if somebody wants

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.129
a party with no clear policy positions, you're.

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:08.980
But unfortunately, in the real world.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:11.469
In the real world, you have to take a position
on something.

00:04:11.469 --> 00:04:12.469
Yeah.

00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:15.689
Speaker 1: And this was kind of an issue when
I interviewed Andrew Yang about the forward

00:04:15.689 --> 00:04:16.730
party.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:23.390
And I said, well, he said everybody's welcome
as long as they believe in two things about

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:24.390
third parties.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:26.270
And I asked him, Well, so are white nationalists.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:27.270
Welcome.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:32.440
And he said, yes, as long as they believe
in those couple of things that imploded.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:36.120
And later on Twitter, he had to backtrack
and say, actually, the white nationalists

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.380
aren't welcome.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.970
I'm just not really getting this.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.220
I want third parties.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:44.750
I want a lot of the things that Andrew Yang
says he wants as well.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:48.470
But I'm not clear that we're getting it through
the forward party.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:53.720
Here's another clip where he was asked about
his kind of unusual tweet about the Mar a

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:56.340
Lago search warrant.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.250
And again, just everything about the answers
is kind of strange.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.520
Speaker 2: Ironic presidential candidate Andrew
Yang.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.240
He recently helped form a new political party
called Forward alongside two former Republican

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.240
officials.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.560
We Should Know for it also happens to be the
title of his recent book.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.050
Andrew, thanks very much for joining us.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:18.220
Can I ask you about something that you tweeted
about the FBI search at Mar a Lago?

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:23.050
You wrote, It looks increasingly like the
FBI raid was to find mishandled classified

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:28.240
documents and not some other DOJ investigation,
which was my first thought.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.760
Mishandling documents doesn't seem like raid
material.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:36.220
It does, however, seem like excellent campaign
material for Trump.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:37.450
And I have to ask you.

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:41.400
It sounds like you're you're letting Trump
off the hook here.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:45.060
Taking top secret documents to your mansion
is okay with you.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:46.820
Are you giving Trump a pass?

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:51.650
Speaker 3: Well, thanks, Jim, is a very disturbing
report you just got from Donie.

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:55.340
And the fact is extremism is on the rise throughout
the country.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:59.330
It's one reason why we think the forward party
is so vital, because we think there needs

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:04.530
to be a broad, positive, unifying third party
movement.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:09.780
But I'm for anything that enhances national
security and the rule of law.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.700
And our hope is that as the attorney general.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:15.170
Speaker 1: I'm for anything that enhances
national security.

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:20.820
I mean, isn't that a cop out like he's he's
basically saying if what happened at Mar a

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.940
Lago enhances national security, then I'm
for it.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:26.730
But if it isn't, I'm against the like it's
a mishmash.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:32.570
Speaker 3: And the DOJ dig in that the American
people get a lot of the answer is that many

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:34.430
of us are eagerly anticipating.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:35.490
Speaker 2: All right.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.980
But are you saying that I don't think you
answered my question.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:42.970
Are you saying that the attorney general had
political motivations with this search at

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:43.970
all?

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.690
Speaker 3: I trust that that all of the DOJ
employees and particularly the FBI agents

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:51.520
are faithful public servants discharging their
their duty.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:58.840
And it it it hurts us all that people are
singling out individuals in the way that Donald

00:06:58.840 --> 00:06:59.840
described it.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:00.840
Yeah.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.910
Speaker 1: So I guess he's against FBI agents
being violently targeted.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:05.910
One last clip.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:08.230
And then I'll give you my thoughts on something.

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:15.220
Speaker 3: Oh, we're for the common sense
consensus view on guns, abortion, climate

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:16.220
change.

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:17.220
But we're not getting a.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:18.220
Speaker 1: What are those views.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:19.470
Speaker 3: And what are those positions?

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.220
And these those things do I mean, those are
just sort.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:24.150
Speaker 2: Of fuzzy, but those are modulus.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:29.310
These like you came up with something in a
focus group, you know, common sense, you know,

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.310
middle of the ground.

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:31.310
That sounds wonderful.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:32.310
That sounds great.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.430
But at the end of the day, don't you have
to take a position on something?

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:36.050
Speaker 3: All.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:40.270
The great thing is the American people know
where we want the country to go and what we

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:41.270
know.

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:42.270
Speaker 1: I don't know that that's true.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:46.760
Speaker 3: That we need a more dynamic, truly
representative system than we're getting right

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:50.300
now, which is why the forward party is growing
so quickly.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.000
Tens of thousands of Americans have signed
up in all 50 states.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:54.660
Speaker 1: Yeah, I don't know.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.200
I mean, tens of thousands.

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:00.620
It doesn't actually sound like very much in
a country of 331 million.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:01.710
So I don't know what to tell you.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:09.790
Guys like this isn't feeling like the electric
injection of a third party.

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:15.419
And in my mind, it's just sort of like, okay,
I know where the Democratic Party stands and

00:08:15.419 --> 00:08:17.460
I know where the Republican Party stands.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:21.430
And I know that although I'm not a Democrat
or a Republican, I'm mostly voting for Democrats

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:23.780
because they're closer to where I stand.

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:29.220
If I'm going to consider the forward party,
I need to know what is their policy position

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:35.649
on guns, on abortion, on taxation, and to
say that it's the common sense consensus view.

00:08:35.649 --> 00:08:39.300
It's not enough for me to say This is the
movement for me.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:43.570
If I'm missing something, if I'm being too
hard on Andrew Yang, let me know.

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:45.959
You can find me on Twitter at David Pakman.

